description:
card.rs:317-332. The intermediate-walk loop:

    for seg in &parts[..parts.len() - 1] {
        let next = current.entry(seg.clone()).or_insert_with(...);
        if let Value::Object(ref mut map) = next {
            current = map;
        } else {
            // Target exists but is not an object — replace with object.
            *next = Value::Object(serde_json::Map::new());
            if let Value::Object(ref mut map) = next {
                current = map;
            } else {
                return;
            }
        }
    }

The inner else-branch repeats the same "if let Value::Object(ref mut map) = next" pattern after just having assigned *next = Value::Object(...). The second match is therefore unreachable-by-construction — yet the code adds a defensive `return` for the impossible branch, which a reader has to mentally check.

This is the textbook "fight the borrow checker" pattern: the author wanted to reassign `current = map` after the replace, the borrow checker refused, so they re-matched. The idiomatic Rust answer is to use Value::as_object_mut() once and let the type system carry the invariant:

    if !next.is_object() {
        *next = Value::Object(serde_json::Map::new());
    }
    let Value::Object(map) = next else { unreachable!() };
    current = map;

…or, cleaner, restructure to not require the post-assignment in the first place (the recursive form fits this naturally; an iterative walk requires the awkward pattern).

Engineering cost: low (correctness is OK), but the code reads as a borrow-checker workaround rather than as the intent. A reader cannot tell whether the inner else is defensive or a real edge case. Combined with finding .4 (which deletes the helper entirely in favor of json_merge_patch), this is one more reason to delete rather than refactor.
